  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match hypertension PRS candidates are present (e.g., PGS001320, PGS002047, PGS002335, PGS004234, PGS004236, PGS004934). Among these, PGS004236 is best supported by the visible evidence as it has strong phenotype alignment (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all indicate hypertension), uses a multi-score aggregation-style construction (PRSsum; favorable vs single-score methods per provided domain guidance), and reports a high full-model AUROC (0.764, 95% CI 0.751–0.777) plus an effect size (OR 2.1, 95% CI 1.99–2.21), with explicit covariates listed. The main limitation is that the reported AUROC is a full-model metric with mediator-heavy covariates (including BMI, smoking, race/ethnic background, study site), making cross-model comparability and PRS-only discrimination uncertain; additionally, several alternative direct-match candidates (e.g., PGS001320 with explicit PRS-only AUROC/R2, and other methods like LDpred2/PolyFun-pred/SBayesR families) remain plausible, so confidence is not high."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000709",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates are present for the target trait (heart failure). PGS000709 is preferred because it is an explicit heart failure score using a multi-PRS/ensemble-style method (snpnet (multi-PRS)), with a large training cohort (UKB; n=223,327) and European development/evaluation, and it reports time-to-event discrimination (C-index=0.635) and an HR per SD of 1.08 (1.06–1.10) with only basic covariates (age time-scale, sex, batch, PCs). However, the overall evidence remains sub-optimal because many competing heart-failure/CHF scores exist with differing endpoints (heart failure vs congestive HF vs incident/prevalent), several report only full-model metrics (often not PRS-comparable), and there is meaningful competition from other modern genome-wide shrinkage/cross-ancestry methods (e.g., PRS-CS/PRS-CSx families) where PRS-only performance is not consistently available for a clean head-to-head comparison in the provided metadata."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002027","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates exist for retinopathy primarily as diabetic retinopathy (PGS000819, PGS001819, PGS002027; plus PGS000862 is labeled diabetic retinopathy but its phenotyping_reported is a diabetes subtype rather than retinopathy). Several other returned models are for age-related macular degeneration, which is not a direct match to generic retinopathy.

Among the diabetic retinopathy models, PGS002027 is the best supported from the visible evidence because it has strong methodological properties (LDpred2, genome-wide shrinkage) and a higher PRS-comparable performance signal than the UKB portability siblings via partial correlation (partial-r=0.0451 vs 0.0366 for PGS001819), with similar endpoint wording and covariate sets (age/sex/PC-style). PGS000819 is disease-focused and genome-wide (LDpred, 3.5M variants) and reports OR=1.12, but has unknown covariates and no PRS-comparable AUC/R2, making it harder to compare directly.

Overall confidence is only Moderate and the outcome is DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL because the target trait is generic 'retinopathy' while the available direct matches are specifically diabetic retinopathy (endpoint narrowing), and none of the candidates provide clear PRS-only AUROC/R2 for the retinopathy endpoint; available metrics are limited (partial-r or modest OR) and not directly comparable across study designs."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002763","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to hip osteoarthritis (or closely synonymous coxarthrosis), but the evidence is insufficient to identify a single clearly best model with high certainty because most candidates lack PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R²) and several report only full-model metrics or single effect sizes.

PGS002763 is preferred because it has (i) exact phenotype alignment (hip osteoarthritis as the reported and phenotyped endpoint), (ii) a methodologically strong genome-wide Bayesian shrinkage approach (PRS-CS) with ~1.05M variants, and (iii) a reasonably strong reported per-SD effect size (OR=1.38, 95% CI 1.31–1.46), which is supportive evidence of discriminative PRS signal. Competing candidates include PGS000967, which is a sparse pan-trait UKB framework score (a known negative study archetype per the provided domain knowledge) despite having explicit PGS-only metrics; and several other genome-wide scores (e.g., PGS004478/PGS004548/PGS004882) with weaker/less complete covariate and performance reporting or more framework-like packaging.

Main limitation: PGS002763 provides no PGS-only AUROC/R² in the visible metadata, and other genome-wide direct-match models exist with broadly similar endpoint alignment but incomplete, non-comparable, or missing performance/covariate details, so the selection remains uncertain."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000471",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches to the target trait (trait_efo = skin carcinoma in situ; phenotyping_reported/trait_reported = Carcinoma in situ of skin) and come from the same publication family with the same ancestry context (EUR-only GWAS/DEV/EVAL) and similar covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1-4), so endpoint fidelity and comparability are aligned across candidates. With no PRS-only AUROC/R2 available (only full-model metrics are shown), differentiation relies on the available within-family performance signals: PGS000471 (lassosum) has the strongest reported full-model discrimination and association (AUROC 0.569; Nagelkerke R² 0.0255; OR per SD 1.401 [1.297–1.513]; top 1% vs rest OR 3.77 [2.24–6.34]), exceeding PGS000470 (AUROC 0.557; R² 0.0154; OR 1.308) and PGS000469 (AUROC 0.524; R² 0.00141; OR 1.09). The main limitation is that the reported AUROC/R² are full-model (covariate-adjusted) rather than PRS-only, which reduces certainty about standalone PRS performance and keeps this as a sub-optimal direct-match recommendation despite being the best-supported among the available direct matches."
}
